{"slug": "raw-and-cooked-tomato-and-herb-salad-with-couscous", "nyt_id": 1014994, "url": "https://cooking.nytimes.com/recipes/1014994-raw-and-cooked-tomato-and-herb-salad-with-couscous", "name": "Raw and Cooked Tomato and Herb Salad With Couscous", "author": "Martha Rose Shulman", "description": "This is an adaptation of a recipe from Yotam Ottlenghi’s cookbook “Plenty.” I liked his idea of combining two different types of couscous, as well as both roasted and fresh tomatoes. The roasted tomatoes coat the couscous and add depth to the overall flavor of the dish.", "servings": {"text": "6 to 8 servings", "min": 6, "max": 8, "yield_type": "servings"}, "time": {"prep_minutes": null, "cook_minutes": null, "total_minutes": 105, "raw": {"total": "PT1H45M"}}, "ingredients": [{"position": 1, "raw": "1 cup couscous", "group": null, "quantity": 1.0, "unit": "cup", "item": "couscous", "preparation": null, "optional": false}, {"position": 2, "raw": "Salt to taste", "group": null, "quantity": null, "unit": null, "item": "salt to taste", "preparation": null, "optional": false}, {"position": 3, "raw": "¼ cup extra virgin olive oil", "group": null, "quantity": 0.25, "unit": "cup", "item": "extra virgin olive oil", "preparation": null, "optional": false}, {"position": 4, "raw": "½ cup Israeli couscous or sorghum, cooked following the package directions, drained and cooled", "group": null, "quantity": 0.5, "unit": "cup", "item": "israeli couscous or sorghum", "preparation": "cooked following the package directions, drained and cooled", "optional": false}, {"position": 5, "raw": "½ pound plum tomatoes", "group": null, "quantity": 0.5, "unit": "lb", "item": "plum tomatoes", "preparation": null, "optional": false}, {"position": 6, "raw": "1 cup finely diced cucumber", "group": null, "quantity": 1.0, "unit": "cup", "item": "finely diced cucumber", "preparation": null, "optional": false}, {"position": 7, "raw": "½ cup basil leaves, cut in slivers", "group": null, "quantity": 0.5, "unit": "cup", "item": "basil leaves", "preparation": "cut in slivers", "optional": false}, {"position": 8, "raw": "2 tablespoons chopped fresh mint", "group": null, "quantity": 2.0, "unit": "tbsp", "item": "chopped fresh mint", "preparation": null, "optional": false}, {"position": 9, "raw": "2 tablespoons chopped fresh tarragon", "group": null, "quantity": 2.0, "unit": "tbsp", "item": "chopped fresh tarragon", "preparation": null, "optional": false}, {"position": 10, "raw": "1 pound heirloom tomatoes in assorted colors", "group": null, "quantity": 1.0, "unit": "lb", "item": "heirloom tomatoes in assorted colors", "preparation": null, "optional": false}, {"position": 11, "raw": "1 tablespoon sherry vinegar (more to taste)", "group": null, "quantity": 1.0, "unit": "tbsp", "item": "sherry vinegar", "preparation": null, "optional": false}, {"position": 12, "raw": "1 garlic clove, finely minced or puréed", "group": null, "quantity": 1.0, "unit": null, "item": "garlic clove", "preparation": "finely minced or puréed", "optional": false}, {"position": 13, "raw": "Basil leaves and cherry tomatoes, halved if desired, for garnish", "group": null, "quantity": null, "unit": null, "item": "basil leaves and cherry tomatoes", "preparation": "halved if desired, for garnish", "optional": false}], "steps": [{"position": 1, "text": "Preheat the oven to 375 degrees. Line a baking sheet with parchment. Place the couscous in a microwave-safe bowl and add salt to taste (about ½ teaspoon) and 2 teaspoons of the olive oil. Toss together. Cover by ½ inch with hot or boiling water. Cover the bowl tightly and let sit 30 minutes, or until all of the water has been absorbed. Fluff with forks and transfer to a large bowl. If you want the couscous to be really fluffy, once it has absorbed the water, cover the bowl with a plate and microwave for 3 minutes. Remove the bowl from the microwave carefully (it will be hot) and carefully remove the plate. Allow to cool slightly. Add the cooked Israeli couscous or sorghum."}, {"position": 2, "text": "Meanwhile, core the plum tomatoes, quarter lengthwise and toss with 1 teaspoon of the olive oil and salt and pepper to taste. Place on the baking sheet in a single layer and place in the oven. Roast 30 minutes and remove from the heat. Allow to cool. Pull off the skins from the wedges (they will be loose and will come off in the salad if you don’t do this now) and toss, along with any juice from the baking sheet, with the couscous. The tomatoes will break down and clump with the couscous. Add the chopped herbs."}, {"position": 3, "text": "Dice the heirloom tomatoes and place in a bowl. Add salt and pepper to taste, the garlic, balsamic vinegar, sherry vinegar, and remaining olive oil and toss together. Add to the couscous and combine well. Mound onto a platter, garnish with basil leaves and cherry tomatoes, and serve."}], "nutrition": {"source": "nyt", "basis": "per_serving", "calories": 203.5, "protein_g": 5.4, "carbohydrates_g": 29.6, "fat_g": 7.2, "saturated_fat_g": 1.0, "unsaturated_fat_g": 0.9, "trans_fat_g": null, "cholesterol_mg": null, "sodium_mg": 342.0, "fiber_g": 3.0, "sugar_g": 2.5, "computed_with": null}, "tags": {"cuisine": [], "diet": [], "course": ["salad"], "meal": [], "occasion": [], "other": ["couscous", "sorghum", "summer", "tomato"]}, "images": [{"url": "https://static01.nyt.com/images/2013/08/23/science/29recipehealth/29recipehealth-superJumbo.jpg", "alt": "", "primary": true}, {"url": "https://static01.nyt.com/images/2013/08/23/science/29recipehealth/29recipehealth-facebookJumbo-v2.jpg", "alt": "", "primary": false}], "rating": {"average": 4.0, "count": 41}, "video_url": null, "comment_count": null, "published_at": "2013-08-29", "scraped_at": "2026-09-06T23:56:22Z", "scraper_version": "1.0.0"}
{"slug": "raw-artichoke-salad", "nyt_id": 1014663, "url": "https://cooking.nytimes.com/recipes/1014663-raw-artichoke-salad", "name": "Raw Artichoke Salad", "author": "David Tanis", "description": "In Italy, a favorite way to serve fresh small artichokes is raw, dressed with oil and lemon. It couldn’t be a simpler presentation, and it’s sensational. Slice the trimmed artichokes as thinly as possible, then season and toss with fruity extra-virgin olive oil and lemon juice. Serve over arugula and add curls of Parmesan.", "servings": {"text": "2 servings", "min": 2, "max": 2, "yield_type": "servings"}, "time": {"prep_minutes": null, "cook_minutes": null, "total_minutes": 15, "raw": {"total": "PT15M"}}, "ingredients": [{"position": 1, "raw": "4 baby artichokes", "group": null, "quantity": 4.0, "unit": null, "item": "baby artichokes", "preparation": null, "optional": false}, {"position": 2, "raw": "1 tablespoon lemon juice", "group": null, "quantity": 1.0, "unit": "tbsp", "item": "lemon juice", "preparation": null, "optional": false}, {"position": 3, "raw": "1 small garlic clove, smashed to a paste, optional", "group": null, "quantity": 1.0, "unit": null, "item": "small garlic clove", "preparation": "smashed to a paste, optional", "optional": true}, {"position": 4, "raw": "Salt and pepper", "group": null, "quantity": null, "unit": null, "item": "salt and pepper", "preparation": null, "optional": false}, {"position": 5, "raw": "3 tablespoons extra virgin olive oil", "group": null, "quantity": 3.0, "unit": "tbsp", "item": "extra virgin olive oil", "preparation": null, "optional": false}, {"position": 6, "raw": "2 handfuls arugula", "group": null, "quantity": 2.0, "unit": "handful", "item": "arugula", "preparation": null, "optional": false}, {"position": 7, "raw": "Parmesan, for garnish", "group": null, "quantity": null, "unit": null, "item": "parmesan", "preparation": "for garnish", "optional": false}], "steps": [{"position": 1, "text": "Remove dark tough exterior leaves from artichokes. Trim stem end and cut off top of each artichoke. Slice artichokes as thinly as possible and place in a small mixing bowl. Add lemon juice and garlic, if using. Season well with salt and pepper, add olive oil and toss to coat."}, {"position": 2, "text": "Put arugula in a shallow bowl or on a platter. Spoon artichokes over greens. Garnish with a few shavings of Parmesan."}], "nutrition": {"source": "nyt", "basis": "per_serving", "calories": 382.0, "protein_g": 15.2, "carbohydrates_g": 33.6, "fat_g": 24.3, "saturated_fat_g": 5.0, "unsaturated_fat_g": 2.4, "trans_fat_g": null, "cholesterol_mg": 9.6, "sodium_mg": 806.3, "fiber_g": 16.5, "sugar_g": 3.2, "computed_with": null}, "tags": {"cuisine": ["italian"], "diet": [], "course": ["appetizer"], "meal": [], "occasion": [], "other": ["arugula", "baby-artichoke", "five-ingredient", "parmesan", "quick"]}, "images": [{"url": "https://static01.nyt.com/images/2013/04/10/dining/10KITCH1_SPAN/10KITCH1-superJumbo.jpg", "alt": "", "primary": true}, {"url": "https://static01.nyt.com/images/2013/04/10/dining/10KITCH1_SPAN/10KITCH1_SPAN-facebookJumbo-v2.jpg", "alt": "", "primary": false}], "rating": {"average": 4.0, "count": 49}, "video_url": null, "comment_count": null, "published_at": "2013-04-10", "scraped_at": "2026-09-06T23:54:04Z", "scraper_version": "1.0.0"}
{"slug": "raw-butternut-squash-salad-with-cranberry-dressing", "nyt_id": 1015137, "url": "https://cooking.nytimes.com/recipes/1015137-raw-butternut-squash-salad-with-cranberry-dressing", "name": "Raw Butternut Squash Salad With Cranberry Dressing", "author": "Mark Bittman", "description": "People aren’t accustomed to eating raw butternut squash, but when it’s grated, it has a wonderful, crunchy quality, and it’s also very pretty. Here, a jumble of grated squash is tossed with a dressing made out of fresh cranberries, honey, orange juice and fresh ginger. It's a lively, fresh twist on the traditional mashed and heavily-buttered treatment.", "servings": {"text": "4 servings", "min": 4, "max": 4, "yield_type": "servings"}, "time": {"prep_minutes": null, "cook_minutes": null, "total_minutes": 20, "raw": {"total": "PT20M"}}, "ingredients": [{"position": 1, "raw": "½ cup fresh or frozen cranberries, picked over and rinsed", "group": null, "quantity": 0.5, "unit": "cup", "item": "fresh or frozen cranberries", "preparation": "picked over and rinsed", "optional": false}, {"position": 2, "raw": "¾ cup orange juice", "group": null, "quantity": 0.75, "unit": "cup", "item": "orange juice", "preparation": null, "optional": false}, {"position": 3, "raw": "1 tablespoon minced ginger", "group": null, "quantity": 1.0, "unit": "tbsp", "item": "minced ginger", "preparation": null, "optional": false}, {"position": 4, "raw": "3 tablespoons olive oil", "group": null, "quantity": 3.0, "unit": "tbsp", "item": "olive oil", "preparation": null, "optional": false}, {"position": 5, "raw": "1 tablespoon honey", "group": null, "quantity": 1.0, "unit": "tbsp", "item": "honey", "preparation": null, "optional": false}, {"position": 6, "raw": "Salt", "group": null, "quantity": null, "unit": null, "item": "salt", "preparation": null, "optional": false}, {"position": 7, "raw": "Black pepper", "group": null, "quantity": null, "unit": null, "item": "black pepper", "preparation": null, "optional": false}, {"position": 8, "raw": "1 butternut squash (about 1 ½ pounds), peeled and seeded", "group": null, "quantity": 1.0, "unit": null, "item": "butternut squash", "preparation": "peeled and seeded", "optional": false}], "steps": [{"position": 1, "text": "Combine the cranberries, orange juice and ginger in a small saucepan over medium-low heat. Cover and cook, stirring occasionally, until the berries have begun to break, 10 minutes or so. Remove from the heat and add the oil, honey and some salt and pepper. Stir until well combined."}, {"position": 2, "text": "Meanwhile, grate the butternut squash by hand or in a food processor. Transfer the squash to a large bowl, add the warm cranberry dressing, and toss to combine everything. Serve warm or at room temperature. (Or cover and refrigerate the salad for up to several hours; bring to room temperature before serving.)"}], "nutrition": {"source": "nyt", "basis": "per_serving", "calories": 200.0, "protein_g": 2.2, "carbohydrates_g": 31.5, "fat_g": 10.4, "saturated_fat_g": 1.5, "unsaturated_fat_g": 1.2, "trans_fat_g": null, "cholesterol_mg": null, "sodium_mg": 577.7, "fiber_g": 4.2, "sugar_g": 12.6, "computed_with": null}, "tags": {"cuisine": [], "diet": [], "course": ["salad-vegetables"], "meal": [], "occasion": [], "other": ["butternut-squash", "cranberry", "fall", "quick", "thanksgiving"]}, "images": [{"url": "https://static01.nyt.com/images/2010/11/18/science/17wellsquash/17wellsquash-superJumbo-v2.jpg", "alt": "", "primary": true}, {"url": "https://static01.nyt.com/images/2010/11/18/science/17wellsquash/17wellsquash-facebookJumbo-v2.jpg", "alt": "", "primary": false}], "rating": {"average": 3.0, "count": 65}, "video_url": null, "comment_count": null, "published_at": "2010-11-09", "scraped_at": "2026-09-06T23:57:41Z", "scraper_version": "1.0.0"}
